{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"new-garden-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Take on a new or neglected garden","description":"Takes someone from a new, inherited or neglected garden to a first-year plan in gated steps - survey, site conditions, keep or clear, layout and priorities, first planting and a routine.","category":"gardening","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","maintain"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","image","preferences"],"output":["plan","checklist","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["new-garden","overgrown-garden","garden-survey","garden-layout","first-year","garden-makeover"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["design-garden-border","plan-vegetable-garden","clear-perennial-weeds","plan-pruning","build-garden-calendar"],"personas":["master-gardener"]},"args":[{"name":"garden","description":"The garden as it is now - size, what is growing, structures, how overgrown, aspect, and your country or region. Photos described in words help.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"wishes","description":"What you want the garden for (food, play, wildlife, relaxing, low maintenance), hours per week, budget, and whether you own or rent.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"survey","file":"steps/01-survey.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/01-survey.md"},{"id":"conditions","file":"steps/02-conditions.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/02-conditions.md"},{"id":"keep-or-clear","file":"steps/03-keep-or-clear.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/03-keep-or-clear.md"},{"id":"layout","file":"steps/04-layout.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/04-layout.md"},{"id":"first-planting","file":"steps/05-first-planting.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/05-first-planting.md"},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/06-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","artifact":"garden/06-routine.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes on a garden the way an experienced garden designer would: look before acting, learn the site's sun, soil and water, keep what is good, clear in the right order, decide the layout before buying plants, plant a manageable first season and set a routine the household can keep. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval.\n\n<garden>\n{{garden}}\n</garden>\n<wishes>\n{{wishes}}\n</wishes>\n\nRules for every step:\n- Use only what the gardener described or confirmed. Ask for missing essentials and mark gaps as [X].\n- Unidentified plants are \"unknown\" until confirmed; never guess an identity that changes what they do (for example removing something that may be valuable or protected).\n- Big trees, structures, walls, electrics and anything near power lines need a professional; say so.\n- If they rent, list what needs the landlord's agreement.\n- No pesticide or herbicide product names or doses; prefer physical and cultural methods.\n- Do not invent prices; list what to price.\n- End each artifact with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"survey","file":"steps/01-survey.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/01-survey.md","text":"# Step 1: Survey what is there\n\n1. Inventory the garden by area: trees, shrubs, borders, lawn, hard surfaces, structures (sheds, fences, walls, steps), water (taps, butts, drains), and anything hazardous (broken glass, rubble, unstable walls, old wiring).\n2. Mark each plant as known, probably (with the features that would confirm it) or unknown.\n3. If the garden is newly taken on, recommend watching it through a season before removing anything not clearly dead or hazardous, because bulbs and perennials appear later.\n4. Flag anything that needs care straight away: hazards, possible invasive species, and trees that may need an arborist.\n\nSections: Inventory, Plants to identify, Hazards and urgent items, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"conditions","file":"steps/02-conditions.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/02-conditions.md","text":"# Step 2: Site conditions\n\n1. Sun: map sun and shade across the day and how it changes by season; ask the gardener to note it if unknown.\n2. Soil: a simple texture test (squeeze test), drainage (a dig-and-fill hole test), pH with a home kit, and signs from existing plants and weeds.\n3. Climate and microclimate: region, frost dates, wind exposure, frost pockets, dry spots by walls, wet hollows.\n4. Water: where water collects and where it is hard to reach with a hose.\n\nSections: Sun and shade map, Soil, Climate and microclimates, Water, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"keep-or-clear","file":"steps/03-keep-or-clear.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/03-keep-or-clear.md","text":"# Step 3: Keep, improve or clear\n\n1. For each item in the inventory, decide: keep, improve (prune, divide, move, repair) or clear, with the reason linked to the conditions and wishes.\n2. Order the clearing: hazards first, then perennial weeds by method and season, then overgrown shrubs with renovation pruning spread over seasons where it suits the plant.\n3. Timing rules: bird nesting season for hedges and shrubs, the right pruning season, and waiting for unknown plants to show themselves.\n4. Waste: what can be composted, what must not be (perennial weed roots, diseased material, invasive species), and disposal options to check locally.\n\nSections: Keep, improve or clear table, Clearing order, Timing, Waste, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"layout","file":"steps/04-layout.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/04-layout.md","text":"# Step 4: Layout and priorities\n\n1. Turn the wishes into zones (seating, play, food, wildlife, storage, paths) placed where the conditions suit them: food and seating in sun, compost and storage in awkward corners, paths along the routes people actually walk.\n2. Sketch the layout in text with rough dimensions.\n3. Split the work into phases by cost and effort: this season, next year, later. Keep the first phase within the hours and budget given.\n4. List what to price and which jobs need a professional.\n\nSections: Zones, Layout sketch, Phases, To price and professionals, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"first-planting","file":"steps/05-first-planting.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"garden/05-first-planting.md","text":"# Step 5: First planting\n\n1. Plan only the first phase: which beds to prepare and how (no-dig with mulch or forking), soil improvement, and plants or seeds matched to each spot's light and soil.\n2. Give a dated planting list for their climate and quantities per bed.\n3. Include quick wins (annuals, salads, bulbs in season) so the garden looks cared for while slower plants establish.\n4. Watering for new plants through the first summer.\n\nSections: Bed preparation, Planting list, Quick wins, Watering new plants, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"routine","file":"steps/06-routine.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","artifact":"garden/06-routine.md","text":"# Step 6: Routine for the first year\n\n1. A weekly routine that fits the hours given: watering, weeding, deadheading, picking, checking new plants.\n2. A month-by-month list of seasonal jobs for their climate, drawn from the earlier steps (pruning, sowing, mulching, lawn, clearing phases).\n3. What to record (what thrived, what failed, where water sat, where sun fell) to plan year two.\n\nSections: Weekly routine, Month-by-month jobs, Notes for year two."}]}
